```
# ============================================================================
# Detection: Anomalous DNS Query Volume/Entropy (Possible DNS Tunneling)
# Detection ID: DE-EF-001
# MITRE ATT&CK: T1048.003 - Exfiltration Over Alternative Protocol: DNS
# Data Source: DNS query logs (Zeek/Sysmon 22)
# Severity: High
# ============================================================================

index=network sourcetype IN ("dns","XmlWinEventLog:Microsoft-Windows-Sysmon/Operational") (EventCode=22 OR sourcetype="dns")
| eval qlen=len(query)
| eval subdomain=mvindex(split(query,"."),0)
| eval entropy=round(((len(replace(subdomain,"(.)(?=.*\\1)","")) / len(subdomain))*100),2)
| bucket _time span=1h
| stats count avg(qlen) as avg_query_length dc(query) as unique_queries by src_ip, dest, _time
| where avg_query_length>50 AND unique_queries>200
| sort -unique_queries
```

## Detection Logic
DNS tunneling tools (iodine, dnscat2, DNSExfiltrator) generate a high volume of long, high-entropy subdomains encoding exfiltrated data chunks. This search flags hosts with abnormal query-length and unique-query-count profiles against a given domain in an hour window.

## False Positive Considerations
CDN/security-vendor domains with legitimately high subdomain cardinality (e.g., telemetry endpoints) — maintain a domain allowlist and exclude before alerting.

## Tuning Notes
Consider adding Shannon entropy calculation via a custom command for more precise scoring; the simplified repeat-character heuristic here is a lightweight approximation suited to SPL-only environments.
